<p>A Hong Kong appeals court has overturned the fraud conviction of Jimmy Lai, the founder of the now-defunct Apple Daily newspaper, in a ruling issued on Thursday (Feb 26). The court said prosecutors failed to prove key elements of the offence, including that Lai made a “false representation,” according to the judgment as described by local reporting. The ruling clears one conviction from Lai’s record, but it does not secure his release: he remains detained in connection with a separate national security case, according to coverage. For many observers, the decision is a reminder that appeals can still correct lower-court outcomes—even as Lai’s broader legal jeopardy continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Q1ljlgj1YPTMMpMg85lIcCch1wT1Acpxj-WLz0Yjg2xGgTr3XJbWkEDoZ-F02L6cIZ2LLwwDpu82P388rg17tFQXizMAesviZUQSGtV7oFbj3AvvOY6YSQCU2TvS6BD-K5hEykafBC9WDw9sWs2OY8NQmgHhPf7kMyiMMQ57Zmvl4dSTnozT6JpZIQLaJi1EMwxFuZ1p-2v292K8T3BTgHm7RLrb574vdOzfV9_EIHr43pwUA38KlsdM7z-4Ul9l6z-JTviIZ2HvQ9UsTw' alt='FILE PHOTO: Media mogul Jimmy Lai Chee-ying, founder of Apple Daily, speaks during an interview in Hong Kong, May 29, 2020.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>2022 sentence: In 2022, Lai received a prison term of five years and nine months after being convicted on two fraud charges—one of which has now been overturned on appeal.</li><li>What the fraud case was about: The appeal centered on allegations tied to a consultancy office arrangement linked to Apple Daily’s parent company, Next Digital; the Court of Appeal’s ruling covered both Lai and another company executive, as described in reporting on the judgment.</li><li>Separate national security case: The appeals decision comes as Lai faces separate national security proceedings; some reports say he was sentenced to 20 years on security-law charges.</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5ykq2ykd6zo'>Hong Kong tycoon Jimmy Lai wins appeal against fraud conviction</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a